Given the high flexibility, mechanical strength and electrical conductivity as well as the excellent electrochemical performance, we assembled a symmetric supercapacitor based on CNF@Ni-HITP nanopapers ( $1.5\:\mathrm{cm}\times2.0\:\mathrm{cm}\times0.005\:\mathrm{cm})$ ） for the evaluation of their potential applications in flexible electrochemical supercapacitor devices. A piece of filter paper, working as the separator, was sandwiched between two identical CNF $@$ Ni-HITP nanopaper electrodes. Two pieces of graphite foil were used as current collectors. A gel prepared by dissolving poly(vinyl alcohol) (PVA) in aqueous KCl was used as the electrolyte. Obviously, the use of aqueous electrolyte offers significant advantages of high rate capability, safety, and environmentally friendliness compared to the organic electrolyte. As expected, the device showed typical double layer capacitive behaviors as ilustrated by the CV and GCD curves (Figure 4a-b, Figure S22).The CV curves of the symmetric device recorded within the potential windows from $0{-}0.4\:\mathrm{V}$ to $0{-}1.4\mathrm{V}$ at a scan rate of $10\mathrm{mVs}^{-1}$ showed nearly rectangular shapes. It should be noted that a pair of broad and inconspicuous redox peaks at a cell voltage of 1.0 and $0.6\mathrm{~V~}$ were observed when operating the device at the potential windows of O-1.2 and $0{-}1.4\mathrm{V}$ ， suggesting the presence of minor faradaic processes. Consistently, the GCD curves showed triangular traces within the potential windows from $0{-}0.4\:\mathrm{V}$ to $0{-}1.4\mathrm{V}$ (Figure S22). However, the device had significantly decreased coulombic efficiency when the potential window was extended above 1.O V (Figure S23).
To evaluate the effects of working potential window on the electrochemical performances, we prepared three identical symmetric devices for comparison studies in varying potential windows $(0{-}0.7\ \mathrm{V},\ 0{-}1.0\ \mathrm{V}$ ，and $0{-}1.4\mathrm{~V~}$ ). As illustrated in Figure 4c, the device showed relatively high areal capacitance of $96~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-2}$ (current density: $0.2~\mathrm{mA}~\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ ）within $0{-}1.0$ V. A high gravimetric capacitances of $141.5\mathrm{F}\:\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ could be reached at a current density of 0.075 A $\mathbf{g}^{-1}$ for the device (Figure S22). As shown in Figure $\scriptstyle4\mathrm{c}$ , the overall device including working electrodes, separator, and current collectors is very thin (a thickness of $0.35~\mathrm{mm}$ ), giving a relatively high volumetric capacitance of up to $2800~\mathrm{mF~cm}^{-3}$ . Accordingly, the maximum energy and power density of the device were $6.5~\mathrm{mW}$ h $\mathrm{cm}^{-2}$ (185.7 mW h cm-3) and 0.013 $\mathrm{mW}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-2}(0.37\ \mathrm{mW}\ \mathrm{cm}^{-3})$ , respectively (Figure S24). Noteworthy, the calculated values of capacitance, energy and power density for the assembled device are comparable to the reported values for state-of-the-art MOF-based supercapacitors.17, 20, 49,50 In addition, it retained over $50\%$ of its capacitance ( $59.6\%$ for 0-0.7 V, $50.1\%$ for $0{-}1.0\mathrm{V}$ ）on increasing the current density from 0.2 to $13\mathrm{\mA\cm}^{-2}$ , which gives higher rate capability than those of devices assembled by c-MOF powders.23,24,26 The electrochemical cycle performance of the device was investigated by GCD cycling experiments at 1 A $\mathrm{g}^{-1}$ . The device showed outstanding stability with no obvious reduction in capacitance after cycling 10o00 times between O and 0.7 V, while only a $10\%$ reduction in capacitance was observed when cycled between O and $1.0\mathrm{V}$ (Figure 4d). Consistently, the CNF $@$ Ni-HITP nanopaper electrode possessed high structural stability as the crystalline structure and nano-morphology were unchanged during the cycling measurements (Figure S25). The high rate capability and stability of the device can be attributed to the integrated nanofibrous structure of CNF $@$ Ni-HITP as mentioned above. In comparison, the device showed slightly higher capacitances at a wider potential window of $0-$ 1.4 V (Figure S22), which resulted in significantly increased energy density and power density (Figure S24). However, the charge-discharge cyclic stability (Figure S26) and rate capability (Figure S22d) were sacrificed by extending the potential windows, which probably can be attributed to the minor faradaic process as revealed from the CV studies (Figure 4a). We anticipated that device optimization by, for instance, replacing the negative electrode with carbon-based materials, using organic or ionic liquid electrolytes, would further extend the working voltage and increase the cycling stability of the device.
In addition to high capacitance and stability, mechanical flexibility is essential for energy storage devices to power flexible electronics, such as foldable phones, curved displays, and wearable devices. Previous studies showed that pressing c-MOF powders into dense and thick pellets could dramaticaly increase the areal and volumetric capacitances of the devices.23,26 However, such devices are rigid and not optimal for use in flexible electronics. In comparison, the CNF $@$ Ni-HITP supercapacitor developed in this study shows excellnt flexibility. Bending (90, $120^{\circ}.$ ） or even folding $(180^{\circ})$ the device has no influence on the CV curves (Figure 4e). Figure 4f shows that a red light-emitting diode (LED) can be powered by the devices in series under different bending and folding deformations (Figure S27). More interestingly, it can be randomly bended, twisted, and folded during working state while maintaining stable output (Supporting Information, Video S2).
## CONCLUSIONS
In summary, continuous c-MOF nanolayers have been successfully fabricated on the surface of CNFs by interfacial synthesis. The obtained hybrid nanofibers are processable allowing further fabrication into freestanding CNF@c-MOF nanopapers, demonstrating high conductivity, hierarchical porosity, mechanical strength and flexibility. The high conductivity and hierarchical porosity facilitate charge transfer and electrolyte transport, which results in excellent electrochemical performances for the CNF $@^{\mathrm{c}}$ -MOF nanopaper electrodes and the assembled supercapacitor. In addition, the supercapacitor shows high stability and mechanical flexibility. The relationship between the nanostructures, physical properties and electrochemical performances of the CNF $@^{\mathrm{c}}$ -MOF nanopaper electrodes indicate that the formation of continuous c-MOF nanolayers is a key factor for the high electrical conductivity of the CNF $@^{\mathrm{c}}$ -MOF nanopaper electrodes and the high stability of the assembled supercapacitor. This study may open up possibilities for synthesis of c-MOF-based materials with optimal structural design for next generation flexible electronics.
## METHODS
Materials. Cladophora cellulose powder was provided by FMC Biopolymer, U.S.A. Sodium bromide (NaBr), 2, 2, 6, 6-tetramethylpiperidine-1-oxyl (TEMPO), sodium hypochlorite solution (NaClO, $13\%$ ), nickel nitrate hexahydrate $(\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{NO}_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O})$ ，nickel acetate hydrate $\mathrm{(Ni(OAc)\it{2}\cdot4H_{2}O)}$ , ammonium hydroxide were purchased from Sigma-Aldrich without further purification. HHTP (2, 3, 6, 7, 10, 11-hexahydroxytriphenylene) was purchased from Tokyo Chemical Industry Co., Ltd. and HITP (2, 3, 6, 7, 10, 11-hexaaminotriphenylene) was purchased from Tensus Biotech (Shanghai, China).
Synthesis of CNF $@$ Ni-HHTP Nanofibers. A solution of $\mathrm{Ni}(\mathrm{OAc})_{2}{\cdot}4\mathrm{H}_{2}\mathrm{O}$ （ $\mathrm{60~mg}$ 0.24 mmol) in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ water was added into a suspension of ion-exchanged CNFs ( $20~\mathrm{mL}$ ,5 mg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ )(Detailed chemical treatments are provided in Supporting Information). Then a solution of HHTP ( $42\mathrm{mg}$ , 0.12 mmol) in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ water was added. The mixture was stirred at $80~^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{~h~}$ and a dark blue suspension was formed. The suspension was filtered on the PVDF membrane. The obtained product was washed by deionized water and acetone for five times, and then dispersed in water to give an aqueous suspension of CNF $@$ Ni-HHTP nanofibers.
Synthesis of CNF $@$ Ni-HITP Nanofibers. A solution of $\mathrm{(Ni(NO_{3})_{2}{\cdot}6H_{2}O}$ （ $\mathrm{66~mg}$ 0.23 mmol) in $10~\mathrm{mL}$ water was mixed with a suspension of ion-exchanged CNFs ( $30~\mathrm{mL}$ ,5 mg $\mathrm{mL}^{-1}$ . Then, a solution of HITP·6HCl ( $(100~\mathrm{mg},0.18~\mathrm{mmol})$ in $25~\mathrm{mL}$ water was added. Thereafter, $3~\mathrm{mL}$ concentrated aqueous ammonia (NH4OH, $14\mathrm{~mol~L^{-1}}$ ）was added. The mixture was stirred at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $2\mathrm{h}$ under an air flow and additional $^{4\mathrm{h}}$ without air flow. The obtained dark suspension was then centrifuged and washed by deionized water for three times.
Fabrication of $\mathbf{CNF}@\mathbf{c}$ -MOF Nanopapers. The aqueous suspension of CNF@c-MOF nanofibers with a concentration of $3\mathrm{\mg\mL^{-1}}$ was vacuum filtrated using the apparatus shown in Figure S2. The obtained nanopapers were mold pressed between two metal plates and dried at $70^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ for $12\mathrm{h}$
Material Characterizations. Powder X-ray diffraction (XRD) patterns were recorded in a Bruker Focus D8 diffractometer with Cu-Kα X-ray radiation $(\lambda{=}1.54\mathrm{~\AA~})$ . Transmission electron microscope (TEM) images were recorded on a TEM instrument (Tecnai, AT02) at an accelerating voltage of $200\ensuremath{\mathrm{\kV}}$ . Scanning electron microscope (SEM) images were collected on a FEG SEM instrument (Zeiss, Leo Gemini 1530) at an accelerating voltage of $3~\mathrm{kV}$ Thermogravimetry analysis (TGA) was performed on a thermogravimetric analyzer (Mettler
Toledo, TGA/SDTA851e) under ${\bf N}_{2}$ or air flow $(60\mathrm{\mL\min^{-1}}$ ) between 25 and $900^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ with a heating rate of $10\ \mathrm{^{\circ}C\ m i n^{-1}}$ ， $\Nu_{2}$ sorption isotherms were measured in a Micromeritics ASAP 2020 surface area and pore size analyzer at $77\mathrm{K}$ . The samples were degassed at $100^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ under a kinetic vacuum $\mathrm{(<10^{-5}~m m H g)}$ for $10\mathrm{~h~}$ prior to the measurements. Pore size distribution were calculated by the Micromeritics ASAP 2O2O software package. The electrical conductivity measurements were carried out on a 4-point-probe station (Advanced Instrument Technology, CMT-SR20ooN). Mechanical properties were measured on a Shimadzu Instrument (AGS-X) at room temperature.
Electrochemical Characterizations. The electrochemical analyses were carried out in a conventional three-electrode set-up in an aqueous 3 M KCl electrolyte. An Ag/AgCl electrode and a platinum wire were used as the reference electrode and counter electrode, respectively. Cyclic voltammetry (CV） and charge-discharge (GCD） curves were recorded on an Autolab/GPES instrument (ECO Chemie, Netherlands). The electrochemical impedance spectroscopy (EIS) measurements were carried out on a CHI 66OD-3 (CH Instruments, Inc., USA). The frequency varied between $100~\mathrm{kHz}$ and 0.01 Hz with an ac amplitude of $5~\mathrm{mV}$ Detailed computational processes are provided in Supporting Information.
Fabrication of Supercapacitors. A symmetric supercapacitor was fabricated by sandwiching a piece of filter paper separator (soaked with PVA/KCl gel electrolyte) with two pieces of identical CNF $@^{\mathrm{c}}$ -MOF nanopaper electrodes ( $(1.5\ \mathrm{cm}\times2\ \mathrm{cm}\times0.005\ \mathrm{cm},26.7$ $26.7\mathrm{mg}$ 。 The PVA/KCl gel electrolyte was prepared by dissolving $1.0\mathrm{g}$ PVA and $0.746\mathrm{gKCl}$ in $10\mathrm{mL}$ deionized water at $90^{\circ}\mathrm{C}$ . The electrodes were immersed in the PVA/KCl gel for 30 minutes. Two pieces of graphite papers were used as current collectors. The total thickness of the device is $0.35\mathrm{mm}$ including two pieces of graphite paper, two pieces of CNF $@^{\mathrm{c}}$ -MOF nanopaper, a piece of separator. CV and GCD measurements of the supercapacitor were conducted on an
Autolab/GPES instrument. Detailed computational processes are provided in Supporting Information.
